      "worked": "For $a=0$, the equation is $|x|=1$, giving the two solutions $x=\\pm1$.\n\nFor $a>0$, consider intersections of $y=|x||1-ax|$ with $y=1$. On $x<0$, the branch $y=ax^2-x$ decreases from infinity to $0$, giving one intersection. On $x>\\frac{1}{a}$, the branch $y=ax^2-x$ increases from $0$ to infinity, giving another.\n\nOn $0\\le x\\le\\frac{1}{a}$, the graph is the downward arch\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=x-ax^2\\\\y&=-a\\left(x-\\frac{1}{2a}\\right)^2+\\frac{1}{4a}.\\end{align*}$$\nIts maximum is $\\frac{1}{4a}$. This arch gives two additional intersections if $0<a<\\frac{1}{4}$, one if $a=\\frac{1}{4}$, and none if $a>\\frac{1}{4}$. Thus exactly two solutions occur for $a>\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nReplacing $a$ by $-a$ reflects the graph across the $y$-axis, so the same count applies for negative parameters. Hence\n$$a<-\\frac14\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a>\\frac14.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
